{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790342205,"nonce":"GeLG2N8KfyjPKrNrinUIA9duQyc=","title":"Collector: a 32 MB floor and 4x growth set the heap of small programs","message":"collector.rs MIN_HEAP is 32 MB and MAX_GROWTH 4 after an unproductive collection, so the budget is max(live x growth, floor). A program with a small live set runs in a 32 MB heap, and one building its data can reach 5x live (records' next budget is 118 MB after 29.5 live).\n\nEvidence (Mac, ZYNTAX_TRACE_GC=1 plus slab census at exit, ZYLUA_LLVM=1 knob runs over 3 interleaved rounds): binary_trees live 4.6 MB in a 36.9 MB heap; op_overload 47 collections reaching 0 bytes with the heap at 32.3 MB; tokenize live 2.2 MB, heap 34.5; closure.lua heap 32.9 over 14,735 collections; pipeline 117 MB live peak with 45 to 60 MB growth overshoot. FLOOR 4 MB: binary_trees footprint 95 -\u003e 79 to 85 but collector 36 -\u003e 156 ms. FLOOR 8 MB: op_overload 76 -\u003e 52 MB but 10 -\u003e 93 ms, pipeline 281 to 298 -\u003e 233 to 237 MB, tokenize 99 -\u003e 149 MB (growth 4x live); GROWTH=1 with it brings tokenize to 113.\n\nSavings: binary_trees, op_overload, tokenize, closure about 25 to 30 MB each; pipeline 45 to 60.\n\nFix: the floor cannot drop alone without slowing binary_trees and op_overload. Cap growth (MAX_GROWTH only while above the floor; an unproductive collection raises the budget additively), and lower the floor once collections of a small live set are cheap (Immix, b94b4cb). Package MEM-gc-heap-return (growth part); floor waits for b94b4cb.","files":null}]}